From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ryan Murray <ryan@aurorasilicon.org>
Date: Sun, 25 Oct 2026 18:11:31 +1100
Subject: [PATCH 1005/1005] filter-graph: follow plugin latency after
 activation

The graph latency is added up once, in impl_activate(), from each node's
latency notify port.  LV2 and LADSPA plugins only write that port from
run(), so at that point it holds 0 on the first activation and the
previous instance's value on every later one.  Nothing reads it again,
so filter-chain keeps publishing a ProcessLatency that is 0 or stale for
as long as the graph runs, and a client that schedules audio by the
stream delay (shairport-sync through pipewire-alsa, for one) plays early
or late by the difference.

Check the latency ports after each process cycle.  When one changes,
signal a main-loop event, add the graph latency up again there and emit
the graph info only when the result moved.  The check is a few float
compares per cycle and the signal is an eventfd write, so the process
path stays real-time safe.  Without LoopUtils nothing changes.

Add a test with a LADSPA plugin that reports 0, then 64 after its first
run, then 128.  The published latency has to follow it, including the
stale value after a new activation, with exactly one info per change and
no event sources left behind by activate and deactivate cycles.

Assisted-by: LLM
Signed-off-by: Ryan Murray <ryan@aurorasilicon.org>
